  "prompt_text": "The figure outlines a transfer process for thin MoS₂ flakes from a growth substrate to a different target substrate. It begins with MoS₂ grown as scattered triangular and polygonal islands on a solid substrate. An inset shows the material’s atomic arrangement, with a honeycomb-like top view and a thin layered side view, emphasizing that it is a very thin sheet.\n\nNext, a polymer support layer is coated over the MoS₂ and the original substrate. The supported film is then placed into what is labeled as 35% ammonia solution. In this liquid, electrostatic repulsion appears to drive delamination, lifting the polymer/MoS₂ film away from the growth substrate without breaking it apart.\n\nAfter separation, the flexible supported film is positioned onto a new target substrate. In the final step, the polymer support is removed, leaving the MoS₂ flakes attached to the target surface in roughly their original shapes and arrangement.\n\nOverall, the image communicates a wet-transfer method for two-dimensional MoS₂: grow it, support it with polymer, use electrostatic repulsion in ammonia to peel it off, then redeposit it onto a desired substrate and clean away the support.",
